{"connection_id": "BC-77e72a6d82ab9bbe2fc7", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.58", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Steadfast work is not in vain", "note": "Paul calls the community steadfast and abundant in the Lord's work, assuring them that their labor is not empty; this counters the commentary's language of human loss with enduring good action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba3bdda9f2bf69269cd2", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Tested faith compared with refined gold", "note": "Peter says tested faith, more precious than perishable gold refined by fire, will result in praise and honor; this gives pressure a refining yield, though by fire rather than squeezing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-595dd337aeaed148071e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith tested like valuable metal", "note": "The commentary describes pressure drawing out an essence of worth; Peter says tested faith is more precious than perishable gold refined by fire, using another physical ordeal to explain enduring value."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dda1ec43e147b583eb71", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.20", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Enduring suffering while doing good", "note": "Peter asks what credit there is in enduring punishment for wrongdoing, then calls it grace to endure suffering while doing good; this tightly joins patient endurance to righteous action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-24b6070b91ef416281ab", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.20", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "endure suffering for doing good", "note": "The commentary couples good action and patience under pressure; Peter distinguishes punishment for wrongdoing from enduring suffering while doing good, so moral value depends on what the pressured person does."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8d9e9150b8a05dca2681", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.14", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Admonish, encourage, help, and be patient with all", "note": "Paul urges the church to admonish the idle, encourage the fainthearted, help the weak, and be patient with everyone; this combines mutual moral care with patience in concrete cases."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ef77c7116c8cd3df1442", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.14", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mutual admonition and patience", "note": "The surah closes with believers counseling one another in patience; Paul directs the community to admonish the idle, encourage the fainthearted, help the weak and be patient toward all, putting counsel and endurance in the same set of relations."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1ff6e7b3d75b72c9cf29", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.15", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Seek the good of one another and everyone", "note": "The church must not repay evil for evil but always pursue good for one another and all; this connects reciprocal ethical counsel to doing good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7eac250cecea569cf29b", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.1.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "comfort passed on from affliction", "note": "The commentary's valuable result of pressure is shared goodness; Paul says comfort received from God in affliction equips sufferers to comfort others, making the product of distress transferable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b863667f9c5619e1cc49", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.1.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "affliction serves others' endurance", "note": "The commentary treats the hard-won essence as goodness given to others; Paul says his affliction serves the community's comfort and salvation, effective as they patiently endure the same sufferings."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c580d7475ef9bbfd15e", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.16", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "outer person wastes, inner person renews", "note": "The commentary places the diminished human beside a preserved inner good; Paul says the outer person is wasting away while the inner person is renewed daily, specifying what can endure inside the fragile vessel of 4:7."}
{"connection_id": "BC-028093f8d94e5b5886b5", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "affliction produces lasting glory", "note": "The commentary says pressure may yield a valuable result beyond the loss it inflicts; Paul says momentary affliction produces an eternal weight of glory, continuing the account of pressed but uncrushed people in 4:8."}
{"connection_id": "BC-57977041f186ac9afc7c", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Treasure in earthen vessels", "note": "Paul places treasure in fragile earthen vessels; this gives a close vessel image for the commentary's bottled essence, while locating the value in the vessel's contents rather than in a bitter remedy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ac9a6cdc4b72f5a73208", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "treasure in earthen vessels", "note": "The commentary places diminished humanity between valuable contents and a holding vessel; Paul describes treasure carried in clay jars so the extraordinary power belongs to God rather than to the fragile holders."}
{"connection_id": "BC-33f36614fd0a1716c38f", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.8", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Afflicted but not crushed", "note": "Paul says the community is pressed yet not crushed; this directly answers the commentary's image of the human as squeezed matter that decreases, with pressure failing to exhaust the people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f0a5f2bd81f0dcb00057", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.8", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pressed but not crushed", "note": "The commentary develops pressure on the human as a possible route to saving essence; Paul says the afflicted are pressed on every side but are not crushed, making the difference between pressure and final loss explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7b8c899a02a45cc9a578", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.9", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Struck down but not destroyed", "note": "Paul distinguishes being struck down from being destroyed; this counters the commentary's equation of human pressure with decrease by describing survival after blows."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4da14d8b4ff8bd8848e7", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.9.10", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God supplies seed and increases the harvest of righteousness", "note": "Paul uses seed, bread, and an increased harvest of righteousness; this connects agricultural yield to the commentary's good essence and gives generosity an ethical harvest image."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-00221d1d00f5720618c2", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.9.11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Enrichment for generosity", "note": "Paul says the community is enriched in every way for generosity; this closely matches the commentary's report that expressed juice serves as a figure for goodness and giving."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f135db4054d1346ae2e8", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.2.21", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "vessel ready for good work", "note": "The commentary's container matters because it keeps something useful; Paul says a cleansed vessel is set apart and ready for every good work, moving from a vessel's condition to the person's capacity for beneficial action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-110340ab35d39c23880f", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.2.24", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The Lord's servant must be patient and able to teach", "note": "Paul describes the Lord's servant as not quarrelsome but patient and able to teach; this joins patient conduct to truthful instruction, though it is pastoral rather than reciprocal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eed143316244e204fed3", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.2.25", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Correct opponents gently toward knowledge of truth", "note": "The servant is to correct opponents gently in hope that God grants them knowledge of truth; this directly joins correction and truth, adding a one-directional teaching scene to the commentary's mutual counsel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aaec69d05b8b7194bd6a", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.4.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Correct, rebuke, and exhort with patience and teaching", "note": "Paul charges Timothy to preach, correct, and exhort with patience and teaching; unlike the commentary's mutual practice, this is a ministerial instruction, but it joins truth-directed correction to patience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c6a9994642000154e040", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.4.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "exhort with patience", "note": "The surah joins giving counsel and sabr; Timothy is instructed to correct, rebuke and exhort with complete patience and teaching, making endurance a quality of the counsel itself, though the instruction is addressed to one teacher."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d35187e455a094f51f5b", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.4.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "life poured out as drink offering", "note": "The commentary's extracted liquid represents the value produced under pressure; Paul, nearing death, describes himself as already being poured out as a drink offering, turning personal loss into an offered gift."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1c404756b01edbf487cc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.12", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Put on patience with other virtues", "note": "Paul tells the community to put on compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness, and patience; this places patience among shared ethical dispositions, extending the commentary's community defined by sabr."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8503e26c64a0c9b333a7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Bear with and forgive one another", "note": "The addressees are told to bear with and forgive one another; this gives the commentary's mutual counsel in patience a concrete practice of forbearance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f52bb1c9cbc6bd530fcb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.16", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Teach and admonish one another with the word", "note": "Paul tells the community to teach and admonish one another with wisdom as Christ's word dwells among them; this is a close communal counterpart to mutual counsel in truth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a49d9a1d1ad85778b461", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.2.10", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Created for good works", "note": "Paul immediately says believers are created in Christ for good works; this keeps the contrast from erasing action and offers a distinct account of deeds as the intended result of salvation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-11d7ce219b1c40a95735", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.2.8", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Salvation as a gift through faith", "note": "Paul says salvation is by grace through faith and is God's gift; set beside the commentary's list of belief and righteous deeds, this frames the ground of rescue differently."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db93bbc19c97f2020bee", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.2.9", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Not from works, so no one may boast", "note": "Paul explicitly denies that salvation is from works, while the commentary includes righteous deeds in the description of those outside loss; the contrast concerns how deeds relate to rescue."}
{"connection_id": "BC-de52db3c475a810c735d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.4.25", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Speak truth each with the neighbor", "note": "Paul commands each person to put away falsehood and speak truth with the neighbor because they belong to one another; this is a direct communal truth-speaking analogue to the commentary's mutual counsel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-34c80a6c3c1ef30ad8b2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bear one another's burdens", "note": "The surah makes patience and counsel reciprocal under human loss; Paul tells believers to carry one another's burdens, giving shared weight a practical communal answer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a7e2e267a7c7ecb66b21", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.9", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Persistence in doing good and harvest in due season", "note": "Paul urges doing good without weariness because a harvest comes in due time if one does not give up; this joins the commentary's righteous action and patience to an agricultural yield."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8bd0118336f7b67634f0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.24", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Stirring one another to love and good works", "note": "The addressees are told to provoke one another toward love and good works; this closely matches the commentary's reciprocal counsel and righteous deeds, though it specifies love rather than truth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-752687b163fa3c61e898", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.24", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mutual prompting toward good works", "note": "The surah names good deeds and reciprocal counsel; Hebrews calls hearers to consider how to stir one another to love and good works, supplying a concrete communal form for the surah's linked actions."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f727a6ec378ac41de385", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.25", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Encouraging one another", "note": "The assembly is to encourage one another as the day approaches; this supplies the reciprocal communal action behind the commentary's repeated mutual exhortation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fbd8420285134010aff8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.25", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "encouraging one another as the day nears", "note": "The commentary's reciprocal counsel is urgent under human loss; Hebrews asks the assembly not to abandon meeting and to encourage one another increasingly as the day approaches, adding persistence in a gathered community to the preceding call to good works."}

