{"connection_id": "BC-07986a518a4a5b7323d2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "endurance and lacking nothing", "note": "The commentary connects sabr to overcoming khusr as diminution; James asks that endurance complete its work so the faithful become complete and lack nothing, joining endurance to the reversal of lack."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8434032000202eb0bdfc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.14", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Can faith without works save?", "note": "James asks whether a person who claims faith but has no works can be saved; this directly tests the commentary's pairing of belief and righteous action, making their relation explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c05e53bd7201379165e5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Faith without works is dead", "note": "James says faith without works is dead; this gives a specific Christian account of why belief and deeds belong together in the commentary's exception from loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0b4bdf440a8df4ae3fad", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Job's endurance and mercy", "note": "The commentary calls sabr a bitter but healing result of pressure; James recalls Job's endurance and the Lord's compassionate outcome, locating patience within a particular sufferer's eventual relief."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cbe7952be47a87d72cd3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.19", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Turning a wanderer back from truth", "note": "James addresses fellow believers and says that whoever turns a brother back from wandering from the truth performs a communal corrective act like the commentary's mutual counsel in truth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9b15fbe1e2d06ff38fbd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.20", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Saving a soul from death", "note": "James adds that bringing the wanderer back saves that person's soul from death; this makes the truth-directed mutual correction a rescue from loss, not merely instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dd2a65316ee8b7dfa374", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The farmer patiently waits for precious fruit", "note": "James tells the community to wait patiently like a farmer for the earth's precious crop; it links patience to delayed yield, though the image is cultivation rather than extraction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d1fd6cb9dbe3f7c80fb4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "farmer waits for precious fruit", "note": "The commentary places patience after pressed produce and scarcity; James asks the community to wait like a farmer for the precious fruit of the earth through its early and late rains, joining endurance, water and eventual yield."}
{"connection_id": "BC-101ef4916b292d49648e", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Pruning a fruitful branch for more fruit", "note": "Jesus says the vinedresser prunes the fruitful branch so it bears more fruit; this gives the commentary's grape and pressure images a contrasting cultivation model where cutting increases future yield."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b5a533b1a9925904da3f", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pruning increases fruitful yield", "note": "The commentary imagines something removed under pressure so that good can emerge; Jesus says the Father prunes a fruit-bearing branch to make it bear more fruit, joining painful subtraction to a larger yield."}

