{"connection_id": "BC-2ca155a0455d765b97fa", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.19.28", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thirst in suffering", "note": "The commentary's dried tongue belongs to one under pressure; at the cross Jesus says he is thirsty, bringing bodily thirst into an account of sustained suffering."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4aade8103d0c3a54d073", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.19.29", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Sour wine lifted to the thirsty Jesus", "note": "The bystanders raise sour wine to Jesus after he says he thirsts; the drink is acidic relief in a death scene, not the beneficial bitter remedy described in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-59c1b18edb50d4fdee69", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.19.29", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sour wine offered for thirst", "note": "The commentary's liquid can be bitter rather than simply refreshing; at the cross sour wine is lifted to the thirsty Jesus on a sponge, coupling distress with an acrid drink rather than plain water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1cb23cadb1e041d5dbc8", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.14", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "drink that ends thirst and becomes a spring", "note": "The commentary links a dry tongue, a relieving drink and a valuable liquid kept within; Jesus says the water he gives ends thirst and becomes a spring within the recipient, turning received relief into a continuing source."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bbe4f060f33f0a6b6e9b", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.7.37", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thirsty invited to drink", "note": "The commentary's dry tongue requires a life-giving drink; Jesus calls anyone thirsty to come to him and drink, offering spiritual relief through the bodily action named in the scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa5387fc878a7fe0e384", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.7.38", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water flowing outward from believer", "note": "The commentary's beneficial extract becomes an outflow to others; Jesus says living water will flow from the believer's inner being, turning received drink into a source rather than a private possession."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e665a24254747f5d28cd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.24", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water for a tormented tongue", "note": "The commentary names a thirst-dried tongue and liquid that could relieve distress; the rich man in torment asks for a fingertip of water to cool his tongue, but the relief he requests is withheld."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b523474a5816d77c155a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.21.19", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "endurance gains life", "note": "The commentary's middle human is in loss and its close calls for sabr; Jesus tells persecuted hearers they will gain their lives through endurance, directly reversing threatened loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-158fc8306b894e030708", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.9.24", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Losing life for Jesus saves it", "note": "Jesus says the one who loses life for his sake will save it; this reverses the commentary's general human loss into a paradoxical self-loss that leads to rescue."}
{"connection_id": "BC-009a1016a9ef11e48fc9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.14.3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "broken jar and poured perfume", "note": "The commentary joins a valuable extract, its vessel and generous outflow; the woman breaks an alabaster jar of costly nard and pours its contents on Jesus, making the opened container the condition of the gift."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3e51370aac644d033f30", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.14.36", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cup of suffering and yielded will", "note": "The commentary makes a cup and its difficult contents part of the pressured person's response; Jesus asks that the cup pass from him but submits to the Father's will, using the vessel for an ordeal faced with endurance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e0fa4d8bd135f6cf2aa3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.15.23", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wine mixed with myrrh is refused", "note": "Mark says wine mixed with myrrh is offered to Jesus and he does not take it; the plant resin in a wine drink recalls the commentary's plant-derived bitter essence, though Mark does not call it bitter or medicinal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-620b3e3a1887d0587fd6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.2.22", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "New wine bursts wineskins", "note": "Jesus says new wine put into old wineskins bursts them and both wine and skins are lost; unlike the commentary's corked bottle retaining a medicinal essence, this vessel fails under pressure and causes loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3337d1b126957264b632", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.9.41", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cup of water given to a disciple", "note": "The commentary says expressed goodness can take the form of a gift; Jesus promises a reward for giving even a cup of water because the recipient belongs to Christ, measuring charity by a concrete drink."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b2b6d1d7a67ed1550bc9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.24.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "enduring to the end saves", "note": "The commentary's final sabr answers the human's peril of loss; Jesus says the one who endures to the end will be saved in a discourse about affliction and betrayal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-98af03efd2e7cb958fb3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.35", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thirst met by giving drink", "note": "The commentary makes liquid relief an act of goodness; the king identifies himself with a thirsty person to whom the righteous gave drink, tying a small bodily gift to the judgment of good action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5a3d361f85a6d1c119ac", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.26.29", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fruit of vine deferred until kingdom", "note": "The commentary recalls renewed pressing after a time of deprivation; Jesus says he will not drink the fruit of the vine again until drinking it new with the disciples in his Father's kingdom, setting present abstention beside future shared wine."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7e40c76fa47fadef0406", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.26.39", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus asks that the cup pass, then submits", "note": "Jesus asks whether the cup can pass but submits to the Father's will; the cup makes suffering a drink-like burden, while the scene adds willing endurance rather than the commentary's extracted medicine."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c6eeefe21050389f7e48", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.26.42", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus accepts the cup he must drink", "note": "Jesus says that if the cup cannot pass unless he drinks it, the Father's will be done; this continuation turns the cup from a requested escape into accepted suffering."}

