{"connection_id": "BC-7d360dc1e01077484863", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.27.34", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Wine mixed with gall is offered and refused", "note": "Jesus is offered wine mixed with gall and refuses it after tasting; this gives a specific bitter drink in a suffering scene, but the text presents no healing benefit like the commentary's medicine."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3ea7721acc446bcd1013", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.27.34", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wine mixed with gall offered and refused", "note": "The commentary's drink can be a bitter product amid suffering; before crucifixion Jesus is offered wine mixed with gall and refuses it after tasting, so an acrid cup accompanies pressure without becoming the healing drink the commentary imagines."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa82ca949c2bdaa48434", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.9.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wine lost if skins burst", "note": "The commentary's stopper and vessel keep the pressed essence from loss; Jesus' wineskin saying says old skins burst under new wine so the wine spills, whereas suitable skins preserve both wine and vessel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e11e5e8e5ccfe73ea3a9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "poured offering joined to others' faith", "note": "The commentary's pressured essence is given away; Paul depicts himself as poured out upon the community's sacrificial service of faith, though the passage supplies neither a pressed plant nor a sealed container."}
{"connection_id": "BC-20751192a5ce573dd56d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.10.10", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Sweet taste turns bitter after eating", "note": "John reports that the scroll tasted sweet and then made his stomach bitter; this locates bitterness after ingestion, unlike the commentary's bitter extract whose bitterness is already the medicinal quality."}
{"connection_id": "BC-91230b1f047ebdb4d976", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.10.9", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Scroll sweet in the mouth, bitter in the stomach", "note": "John is told to eat the scroll, which will be sweet in his mouth but bitter in his stomach; the two-stage taste gives the commentary's swallowed bitter medicine a distinct prophetic counterpart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5efb347b3bcb9628510b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.14.12", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Saints' endurance, God's commands, and faith in Jesus", "note": "The verse defines the saints by endurance, keeping God's commands, and faith in Jesus; this closely gathers patience, faithful action, and belief in the kind of people the commentary exempts from loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-abd60f0b54ae3bc4a3c3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.14.19", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Earth's grapes thrown into the winepress of wrath", "note": "The angel gathers the earth's grapes and throws them into God's great winepress; this directly turns the commentary's pressing that yields good essence into an apocalyptic harvest of wrath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5f070cf7480576f4cd1f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.14.19", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grapes cast into wrath's winepress", "note": "The commentary's press can yield goodness for people; Revelation has an angel gather the earth's grape harvest into the great winepress of divine wrath, giving the same extraction scene a punitive end."}
{"connection_id": "BC-47aa62f5859e571addd4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.14.20", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Winepress trodden; blood flows out", "note": "The press is trodden outside the city and blood flows from it; this specifies the destructive yield of the preceding winepress, in contrast to the commentary's useful extracted juice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba6f01cbdd9cd19b38ab", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.14.20", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blood flows from the trodden press", "note": "The commentary treats the outflow from pressing as a possible valuable essence; Revelation's next verse says blood flows from the winepress after it is trodden, specifying a violent extract rather than a remedy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c259e02520a155ff076", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water of life given freely", "note": "The commentary links a treasured liquid with giving and thirst; Revelation invites the thirsty to take the water of life without payment, making the valuable drink an unrestricted gift."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3cae991faff7a7ac8d24", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.8.11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Wormwood makes waters bitter and kills many", "note": "The star named Wormwood makes the waters bitter and many die from them; this directly reverses the commentary's bitter plant extract as medicine by making bitter liquid lethal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c0d2ba965c65413a7bfe", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.8.11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bitter water brings death", "note": "The commentary calls the bitter extract a medicine; Revelation says waters made bitter by Wormwood kill many drinkers, a concrete reversal in which bitter liquid is harmful rather than curative."}
{"connection_id": "BC-444fac8749039467ca94", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.12.20", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "food and drink given to an enemy", "note": "The commentary interprets beneficial liquid as generosity under pressure; Paul repeats the instruction to feed a hungry enemy and give a thirsty enemy drink, carrying the concrete act from Proverbs into counsel to a Christian community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-117af5d49a2c55119039", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.12.21", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "good overcomes evil after water gift", "note": "The commentary says a squeezed essence can become goodness given away; after directing food and drink to an enemy in 12:20, Paul says to overcome evil with good, naming the moral effect of that costly gift."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3397043e50d78169752b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.2.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Perseverance in doing good leads to eternal life", "note": "Paul describes those who persist in doing good and seek glory and immortality as receiving eternal life; this combines the commentary's good action and patience in a salvific outcome."}
