{"connection_id": "BC-60f25f0b01ebb6724a6e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.5.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Endurance produces tested character and hope", "note": "Paul says endurance produces tested character and hope; this continues the commentary's image of a beneficial quality formed through pressure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8f94be4645ff81d9c4ec", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.5.5", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's love poured into hearts", "note": "Paul says God's love has been poured into believers' hearts; the commentary specifically notes Qur'anic patience poured over people, and this supplies a Christian liquid metaphor for an inward virtue, while naming love rather than patience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff47704cf72a4c7d024a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.5.5", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "love poured into hearts", "note": "The commentary develops sabr as something poured over threatened people; in Paul's sequence after tribulation and endurance, God's love is poured into believers' hearts through the Holy Spirit, giving a distinct beneficial liquid image to what follows pressure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-85e92b11fb0e2fac1b96", "ref": "Shepherd of Hermas, Mandate 5.1", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sweet patience beside bitter remedy", "note": "The commentary calls the sap named sabr a bitter medicine; this mandate presents patience as sweet and valuable in moral struggle, offering a contrary taste for the virtue. The precise subsection within Mandate 5.1 needs verification."}
