{"connection_id": "BC-c062f249fdfe39986bd6", "ref": "WLC:2Kgs.4.41", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remedy added to dangerous stew", "note": "The commentary's bitter extract is a medicine; Elisha adds flour to the pot after the prophets fear death in the stew, and the food becomes harmless. The bridge is a healing substance in a vessel, though the passage does not call the flour bitter or describe squeezing it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-33db60beff4c999c588e", "ref": "WLC:2Kgs.4.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Oil fills every borrowed vessel and then stops", "note": "The widow's oil keeps filling containers until there is no vessel left, when the flow stops; it gives a precise parallel to liquid collected and held by a vessel, with provision released rather than a corked medicine."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d5c6fe778bb6025e6809", "ref": "WLC:Amos.9.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Treader of grapes overtaken by the sower; hills drip wine", "note": "Amos pictures restoration so abundant that grape-treading overlaps sowing and mountains drip sweet wine; this makes pressing the sign of relief and plenty, as in the commentary's post-scarcity year."}
{"connection_id": "BC-93197457f77127df784d", "ref": "WLC:Amos.9.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mountains dripping sweet wine", "note": "The commentary's post-scarcity year is marked by pressing again; Amos pictures restored abundance so great that the mountains drip wine and the hills flow with it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-35d0630babfb698a6fd4", "ref": "WLC:Dan.5.27", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "خ س ر / Aramaic חסר, table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary calls human khusr a diminution; Daniel's verdict says the king has been weighed and found lacking, making deficiency a judgment on a person rather than only a reduced quantity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-96d659f1bc634da0840f", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Commentary label root ء ن س; Aramaic אנש, table sound correspondence only and unverified", "note": "Daniel sees one like a bar enash, a humanlike figure who receives dominion; this contrasts the commentary's collective humanity with a singular exalted use of the related Aramaic word."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5746ac58ae412ae4487c", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء ن س / Aramaic אנש, table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's al-insan is exposed to loss; Daniel sees one like a human being coming with heaven's clouds before the Ancient of Days, using the Aramaic cognate for a humanlike figure poised for a different outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2c85e1616ef58f8ce1f7", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.14", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "dominion given to humanlike figure", "note": "The commentary's human stands under the sentence of loss; the figure described as humanlike in 7:13 receives lasting dominion and a kingdom here, completing that scene with an opposite destiny. The Aramaic אנש occurs in the preceding verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a35db807e7ed943dc3da", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Commentary label root ع ص ر; Hebrew עצר, table sound correspondence only", "note": "The Hebrew verb עצר closes the heavens so rain and yield cease; this restraint is the agricultural inverse of the commentary's squeezing that releases juice and, in its Yusuf comparison, marks relief after scarcity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b83dc3d7d51add69e8e2", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ع ص ر / Hebrew עצר, table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's pressure and dry tongue are tied to withholding liquid; Deuteronomy warns that God may shut the heavens so no rain falls, using עצר for held-back water that brings loss of the land's produce."}
{"connection_id": "BC-de4c0804f238698b3b97", "ref": "WLC:Deut.2.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Commentary label root خ س ر; Hebrew חסר, table sound correspondence only", "note": "Moses says Israel lacked nothing during the wilderness years; the cognate sense of lack gives a direct counter-reading to the commentary's human placed in decrease and loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d547aa68cc84074f93d", "ref": "WLC:Exod.15.23", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bitter water after thirst", "note": "The commentary calls the expressed sabr an unpleasant medicine; after thirst in the wilderness Israel finds water at Marah but cannot drink it because it is bitter, so the desired liquid initially fails to relieve need."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3b00fae5b25917254fc6", "ref": "WLC:Exod.15.25", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Marah's bitter water and the wood", "note": "At Marah the people cannot drink bitter water until Moses throws a tree into it and it becomes sweet; this reverses the commentary's bitter tree extract, which remains bitter yet is named medicine."}
{"connection_id": "BC-46a116b9649b3523c3dc", "ref": "WLC:Exod.15.25", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bitter water made drinkable", "note": "The commentary moves from an acrid extract to healing relief; Moses is shown a tree to put into Marah's bitter water, and the water becomes drinkable through an intervention within the liquid itself."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5197b4504bfb4a83835b", "ref": "WLC:Exod.15.26", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "YHWH as healer", "note": "After the bitter-water episode, YHWH identifies himself as Israel's healer; this makes explicit the healing frame that the commentary gives to bitter sabr, though the remedy and bitterness work differently."}
