{"connection_id": "BC-3cd75f770ee9fa8f2b0f", "ref": "WLC:Gen.40.11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "prisoner's grapes pressed into cup", "note": "The commentary recalls the prisoner's dream of squeezing grapes; in Joseph's prison the cupbearer presses the grapes into Pharaoh's cup, joining confinement, extracted drink and service in one scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ea0971c1b1a806370935", "ref": "WLC:Gen.40.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "cup restored after prison dream", "note": "The commentary reads pressing after hardship as a sign of relief; Joseph interprets the cupbearer's pressing dream as restoration to his former office in three days, while the cupbearer rather than Joseph receives this immediate relief."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1b2ea984706afe11799a", "ref": "WLC:Gen.41.30", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Seven years of famine after seven years of plenty", "note": "Joseph's dream interpretation predicts seven lean years after abundance; it parallels the commentary's sequence of scarcity and eventual relief while preserving a different order from its pressing year."}
{"connection_id": "BC-288a009dc9503c565753", "ref": "WLC:Gen.41.35", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "stored food during abundance", "note": "The commentary joins post-famine pressing to preserving the extracted good; Joseph orders grain stored under Pharaoh during the good years so that produce can outlast the scarcity ahead."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8f391454839789f4a331", "ref": "WLC:Gen.41.36", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "stored food prevents death in famine", "note": "The commentary connects the return of pressing after lean years with keeping valuable produce; Joseph says the stored food will sustain Egypt through seven famine years so the land will not perish, stating the life-saving purpose of the storage ordered in the preceding verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-644a32feb46333b878ea", "ref": "WLC:Gen.41.49", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Commentary label root ص ب ر; Hebrew צבר, table sound correspondence only", "note": "The Hebrew verb צבר describes Joseph piling up grain beyond measure; beside the commentary's corked bottle retaining squeezed essence, the narrow connection is accumulation and storage, not a shared sense of patience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9cc19d296448757bdba8", "ref": "WLC:Gen.41.49", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ص ب ر / Hebrew צבר, table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's sabr root names the bitter extract and the stopper that keeps it; Genesis says Joseph heaped up grain beyond counting before famine, using צבר for a different act of gathering and preserving what pressure will make valuable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-50781282c77ed4b04bcf", "ref": "WLC:Gen.41.56", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Storehouses opened during famine", "note": "When famine covers the land, Joseph opens the storehouses; this connects the commentary's retained essence in a closed bottle with stored provision later released to sustain people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0f995f9435cbec5c2e31", "ref": "WLC:Gen.41.56", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "granaries opened during famine", "note": "The commentary's pressed and stored good becomes relief when want arrives; as famine spreads, Joseph opens the storehouses and sells grain to Egyptians, bringing the preserved supply into use rather than leaving it sealed away."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b228c4d095b2085b1c2b", "ref": "WLC:Gen.50.20", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "evil converted into preservation of life", "note": "The commentary imagines pressure on a person yielding a good that others receive; Joseph tells his brothers that the evil they intended against him was turned toward preserving many lives, interpreting the outcome of his imprisonment and food administration."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e67fefcc0c59431a92da", "ref": "WLC:Isa.16.10", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "winepress treading silenced", "note": "The commentary makes renewed pressing a sign that scarcity has ended; Isaiah's judgment removes joy from the vineyards and silences the treader in the winepress, so the same action's absence signals loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ce339e6e5c2627bf95a5", "ref": "WLC:Isa.41.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Tongue fails for thirst; water is promised", "note": "The needy have no water and their tongue fails from thirst; this closely matches the commentary's dried tongue, while Isaiah's promised springs reverse depletion with relief."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ad3e0edeb2b420658dbf", "ref": "WLC:Isa.41.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thirsting poor with dry tongues", "note": "The commentary's pressed, dry tongue needs liquid relief; Isaiah describes needy people seeking water with none available and tongues parched with thirst, before God's promise to answer them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-16418811203ef44ab67b", "ref": "WLC:Isa.5.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "A winepress prepared for grapes but wild grapes produced", "note": "The vineyard owner builds a winepress expecting good grapes but finds wild grapes; the image makes the commentary's generous essence contingent on a yield that can instead disappoint."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1f470f6b7ba2df492490", "ref": "WLC:Isa.5.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "winepress and bad grapes", "note": "The commentary expects good essence from pressing; Isaiah's vineyard owner prepares a winepress but gets bad grapes from carefully tended vines, reversing the hoped-for product before the vineyard's judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fd7249d284e3a3dd1b3f", "ref": "WLC:Isa.5.7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "justice expected, bloodshed found", "note": "The commentary gives the pressed extract a moral sense of goodness; Isaiah explains his vineyard's failed fruit as Israel's failure to yield justice and righteousness, clarifying why a harvest image can judge a human community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-02c963e31d75102ed16e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.10", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "self offered to the hungry", "note": "The commentary turns expressed essence into generosity; Isaiah asks a person to give of himself to the hungry and satisfy the afflicted, after which light rises from darkness, connecting costly giving to relief."}
{"connection_id": "BC-89f7104aeb27d566fb55", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "watered garden in dry places", "note": "The commentary's dried tongue and beneficial outflow invite a restoration image; Isaiah promises guidance and satisfaction in parched places until the person is like a watered garden and unfailing spring."}
{"connection_id": "BC-18633cbd04d646241b95", "ref": "WLC:Isa.63.3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Winepress trodden in wrath; blood on garments", "note": "The divine speaker treads the press in anger and blood stains his clothing, a direct counter-image to the commentary's extracted juice as good and generous giving."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c2c25ec1531bed53f129", "ref": "WLC:Isa.63.3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "winepress trodden in anger", "note": "The commentary's pressing produces a potentially healing good; Isaiah's solitary winepress treader makes red garments an image of wrath against peoples, a specific contrary use of the same action."}

