{"connection_id": "BC-8ca2f8a94191c0741845", "ref": "WLC:Jer.2.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Living fountain; cracked cisterns", "note": "The commentary ends with a sealed bottle that retains its medicinal essence, whereas Jeremiah's chosen cisterns are cracked and cannot hold water, making failed containment a charge against Judah."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8abf73e5164e759c2dbb", "ref": "WLC:Jer.2.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "broken cisterns unable to hold water", "note": "The commentary's vessel keeps its valuable liquid from loss; Jeremiah contrasts the living-water source with cracked cisterns that cannot hold water, making a failed container an image of a people's self-inflicted deprivation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c73f1b30e7b1a63d8fd", "ref": "WLC:Jer.32.14", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "jar preserves a future claim", "note": "The commentary's stopper and vessel keep something valuable for later; Jeremiah orders purchase documents put in an earthenware jar so they survive many days during Jerusalem's siege, preserving a sign of future restoration."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1cc1251928425cef9ec8", "ref": "WLC:Jer.48.11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wine left on its lees and not poured between vessels", "note": "Moab's undisturbed wine retains its flavor because it has not been poured from vessel to vessel; the commentary's cork likewise preserves a liquid, but Jeremiah makes stillness a figure for complacency."}
{"connection_id": "BC-225766ac5330f089a2fa", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.1", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "frail human full of trouble", "note": "The commentary's human stands in diminishment between pressure and relief; Job says a person born of woman has few days and is full of trouble, sharpening the vulnerability of the one being pressed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4787a629210afa5d6db0", "ref": "WLC:Job.32.19", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wine with no vent; new wineskins ready to burst", "note": "The commentary's cork holds squeezed liquid in a bottle; Elihu instead likens unspoken words to fermenting wine with no vent, so the same containment intensifies pressure toward rupture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-55843974c0ad60278448", "ref": "WLC:Job.7.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Commentary label root ء ن س; Hebrew אנש, regular sound correspondence and BDB Arabic-cognate note", "note": "Job asks what אנוש, a mortal human, is that God gives him such attention; it lends the commentary's generic human between pressure and loss a specific mortal and vulnerable register."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d3a77a04b419eb0b272", "ref": "WLC:Job.7.17", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء ن س / Hebrew אנש, table sound correspondence with BDB cognate note", "note": "The commentary places the human being under pressure; Job asks what frail human is that God magnifies him and sets his attention on him, using אנוש to turn divine notice into an anguished question amid suffering."}
{"connection_id": "BC-969f07260a49e5112b3b", "ref": "WLC:Job.7.18", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human tested every moment", "note": "The commentary's human undergoes pressure; Job continues his question about the אנוש of 7:17 by saying God tests him every moment, making divine attention feel like sustained examination. The root itself is in the preceding verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bbb11adbf6dcdef07475", "ref": "WLC:Joel.1.14", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ع ص ر / Hebrew עצר, table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's opening root evokes pressure and its close calls people to counsel one another; Joel calls a solemn assembly, using עצרה, and gathers elders and inhabitants to cry to God amid a ruined harvest, turning held-together people toward a shared answer to scarcity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5950910671648a029409", "ref": "WLC:Joel.2.24", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "vats full after devastation", "note": "The commentary recalls renewed pressing after scarcity; Joel promises threshing floors full of grain and vats overflowing with wine and oil as the land recovers from devastation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-049fa39acfb6121c82ec", "ref": "WLC:Joel.4.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Ripe harvest; vats overflowing with wickedness", "note": "The harvest press overflows because the nations' wickedness is great; the shared pressing and yield imagery makes the commentary's beneficial extract read against a judgment harvest."}
{"connection_id": "BC-92fe4a1055961ebec285", "ref": "WLC:Joel.4.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "full winepress as judgment", "note": "The commentary's full press can signify recovered abundance; Joel instead calls for the winepress to be trodden because wickedness is great, making the overflowing vats an image of judgment. This is WLC's Hebrew Joel 4:13."}
{"connection_id": "BC-df97fff89dc73ed0d0ed", "ref": "WLC:Judg.6.11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Gideon threshes wheat in a winepress to hide it", "note": "Gideon works grain in a winepress while hiding from Midianite raiders; the press belongs to threatened subsistence here, unlike the commentary's press as the sign that scarcity has ended."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a73ab9557b604e985a18", "ref": "WLC:Judg.6.11", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wheat hidden in a winepress", "note": "The commentary locates pressing beside confinement and scarcity; Gideon beats out wheat inside a winepress to keep it from Midian, using the grape vessel as a hiding place for food under oppression rather than as a source of drink."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d3e424370336150bd931", "ref": "WLC:Judg.9.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wine gladdens God and humans", "note": "In Jotham's fable the vine says its wine gladdens God and people; this concretely parallels the commentary's report that Arabs make expressed juice a figure for good and generosity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2f08121e718a29dedace", "ref": "WLC:Lam.1.15", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Judah trodden as grapes in a winepress", "note": "The lament says the Lord has trodden Judah like grapes in a press; the commentary's human squeezed into loss becomes a crushed people here, and the press yields devastation rather than a useful essence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-267ebfc959ff146d5be2", "ref": "WLC:Lam.2.19", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pour heart like water", "note": "The commentary recalls patience poured on people facing danger; Lamentations tells Jerusalem to pour out her heart like water before the Lord for children fainting from hunger, joining a liquid prayer to communal deprivation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a47f060b9cda824d1f1c", "ref": "WLC:Lam.3.15", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Bitterness and wormwood as affliction", "note": "The speaker says he has been filled with bitterness and wormwood; the plant bitterness is imposed misery, a counter-reading to the commentary's painful but beneficial remedy."}

