+{"root":"ء ن س","decision":"used","hebrew":["אנש"],"reason":"BDB cites an Arabic cognate for Hebrew אנש; WLC Ps.8.5 uses אֱנוֹשׁ for mortal humanity. Aramaic אנש also means human (Dan.7.13), whereas Hebrew and Aramaic אנס mean compulsion or oppression (Esth.1.8; Dan.4.6) and do not illuminate the page.","paragraphs":[11],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-002"]}
+{"root":"خ س ر","decision":"used","hebrew":["חסר"],"reason":"Hebrew חסר and Aramaic חסר denote lack or diminution (Ps.8.6; Dan.5.27); the relation to Arabic خ س ر is sound correspondence only, not a documented loan. Hebrew חשר means gathering or a wheel hub (2Sam.22.12; 1Kgs.7.33) and is a semantic false friend here.","paragraphs":[11],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-002"]}
+{"root":"ص ب ر","decision":"used","hebrew":["צבר"],"reason":"Hebrew צבר means to heap or accumulate, especially Joseph's grain in Gen.41.35 and 41.49; the proposed tie to Arabic ص ب ر rests on sound correspondence only. It offers a precise storage comparison while lacking the Arabic senses of patience, bitter extract and vessel stopper.","paragraphs":[12],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-003"]}
+{"root":"ع ص ر","decision":"used","hebrew":["עצר"],"reason":"Hebrew עצר restrains rain in Deut.11.17 and 1Kgs.8.35 and names bodily closure in Prov.30.16; its relation to Arabic ع ص ر is sound correspondence only. No checked use means pressing grapes or extracting juice, so the annotation marks a semantic boundary.","paragraphs":[10],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-001"]}
