+{"connection_id":"BC-96d659f1bc634da0840f","ref":"WLC:Dan.7.13","status":"rejected","reason":"Aramaic בר אנש names a singular humanlike visionary figure, whose exaltation is distant from the paragraph's generic person under loss.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.7.13"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-5746ac58ae412ae4487c","ref":"WLC:Dan.7.13","status":"rejected","reason":"The humanlike figure is a valid Aramaic lexical occurrence, but the note's general al-insan comparison is made more directly by Hebrew אנוש in Psalm 8.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.7.13"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-2c85e1616ef58f8ce1f7","ref":"WLC:Dan.7.14","status":"rejected","reason":"Dominion belongs to the singular vision figure described in 7:13; this verse does not itself use אנש or describe ordinary humanity's loss.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.7.14"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a35db807e7ed943dc3da","ref":"WLC:Deut.11.17","status":"accepted","reason":"The Hebrew עצר restrains the sky's rain and halts produce, a well constrained lexical contrast to Arabic squeezing and liquid release; the connection is sound-only.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.11.17","WLC:Prov.30.16"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-b83dc3d7d51add69e8e2","ref":"WLC:Deut.11.17","status":"accepted","reason":"This repeat accurately links the held-back rain to agricultural loss, while the annotation states that the Hebrew verb does not mean pressing grapes.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.11.17","WLC:Prov.30.16"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-de4c0804f238698b3b97","ref":"WLC:Deut.2.7","status":"rejected","reason":"Israel lacked nothing in the wilderness, but the positive provision scene has no extraction; Psalm 8 more tightly joins the human term to the lack root.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.2.7"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-2d547aa68cc84074f93d","ref":"WLC:Exod.15.23","status":"accepted","reason":"Marah's water is explicitly bitter and undrinkable, setting up the selected tree-and-healing contrast to bitter medicinal sabr.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.15.23","WLC:Exod.15.25","WLC:Exod.15.26"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-3b00fae5b25917254fc6","ref":"WLC:Exod.15.25","status":"accepted","reason":"A tree is placed in bitter water and the water sweetens; the annotation explicitly distinguishes this from a remedy that remains bitter.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.15.23","WLC:Exod.15.25","WLC:Exod.15.26"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-46a116b9649b3523c3dc","ref":"WLC:Exod.15.25","status":"accepted","reason":"The text shows bitterness changed within the liquid through the tree; this distinct mechanism is stated in the selected note.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.15.23","WLC:Exod.15.25","WLC:Exod.15.26"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-5197b4504bfb4a83835b","ref":"WLC:Exod.15.26","status":"accepted","reason":"God identifies himself as healer immediately after Marah, supplying the healing frame of the selected bitter-water scene.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.15.23","WLC:Exod.15.25","WLC:Exod.15.26"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-7f7bd08b0f57ee018ef8","ref":"WLC:Exod.15.26","status":"accepted","reason":"The second healing proposal is verified in the verse and expressed once with its bitter-water context.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.15.23","WLC:Exod.15.25","WLC:Exod.15.26"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-dea2ca008199d7abcba0","ref":"WLC:Gen.18.28","status":"rejected","reason":"The Hebrew root חסר marks five righteous people missing from a count; this numerical subtraction does not illuminate a pressed human or extracted remedy.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.18.28"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-7f1593a657956492d2cd","ref":"WLC:Gen.20.18","status":"rejected","reason":"עצר closes wombs in Abimelech's house, but the selected Proverbs 30:16 is a more compact bodily-closure use beside unsated water and does not imply an actual bottle stopper.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.20.18"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-5277449d582581c1a9ab","ref":"WLC:Gen.40.11","status":"accepted","reason":"The prisoner's dream explicitly says he squeezed grapes into Pharaoh's cup, the direct narrative counterpart to Qur'an 12:36.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.40.10","WLC:Gen.40.11","WLC:Gen.40.12","WLC:Gen.40.13"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-PRL-001"]}

