@@
 {"connection_id":"BC-5277449d582581c1a9ab","ref":"WLC:Gen.40.11","status":"accepted","reason":"The prisoner's dream explicitly says he squeezed grapes into Pharaoh's cup, the direct narrative counterpart to Qur'an 12:36.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.40.10","WLC:Gen.40.11","WLC:Gen.40.12","WLC:Gen.40.13"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-PRL-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-3cd75f770ee9fa8f2b0f","ref":"WLC:Gen.40.11","status":"accepted","reason":"The second proposal's grapes, pressing, and Pharaoh's cup are all explicit in the same prison dream and represented once.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.40.10","WLC:Gen.40.11","WLC:Gen.40.12","WLC:Gen.40.13"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-PRL-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-ea0971c1b1a806370935","ref":"WLC:Gen.40.13","status":"accepted","reason":"Joseph interprets the cupbearer's dream as return to office in three days; the annotation states that specific hopeful result, not Joseph's immediate release.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.40.10","WLC:Gen.40.11","WLC:Gen.40.12","WLC:Gen.40.13"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-PRL-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-1b2ea984706afe11799a","ref":"WLC:Gen.41.30","status":"accepted","reason":"Seven famine years follow the years of plenty in Genesis; the annotation keeps that order and notes that this account lacks Qur'an 12:49's later pressing year.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.41.30","WLC:Gen.41.35","WLC:Gen.41.36","WLC:Gen.41.49","WLC:Gen.41.56"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-288a009dc9503c565753","ref":"WLC:Gen.41.35","status":"accepted","reason":"Joseph explicitly orders good-year food accumulated and guarded, grounding the storage comparison and the צבר root's scene.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.41.30","WLC:Gen.41.35","WLC:Gen.41.36","WLC:Gen.41.49","WLC:Gen.41.56"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-8f391454839789f4a331","ref":"WLC:Gen.41.36","status":"accepted","reason":"The stored grain is explicitly for sustaining the land during seven famine years, the purpose described in the annotation.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.41.30","WLC:Gen.41.35","WLC:Gen.41.36","WLC:Gen.41.49","WLC:Gen.41.56"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-644a32feb46333b878ea","ref":"WLC:Gen.41.49","status":"accepted","reason":"The opened Hebrew ויצבר means Joseph heaped grain; the sound correspondence to ص ب ر is marked as unverified and the different meaning stated.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.41.30","WLC:Gen.41.35","WLC:Gen.41.36","WLC:Gen.41.49","WLC:Gen.41.56"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-9cc19d296448757bdba8","ref":"WLC:Gen.41.49","status":"accepted","reason":"This repeats the valid grain-heaping word; the annotation limits the lexical comparison to storage and denies a Hebrew sense of patience or bitter sap.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.41.30","WLC:Gen.41.35","WLC:Gen.41.36","WLC:Gen.41.49","WLC:Gen.41.56"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-50781282c77ed4b04bcf","ref":"WLC:Gen.41.56","status":"accepted","reason":"Joseph opens the stored supply when famine covers the land; the annotation expresses release of what was heaped in good years.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.41.30","WLC:Gen.41.35","WLC:Gen.41.36","WLC:Gen.41.49","WLC:Gen.41.56"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-0f995f9435cbec5c2e31","ref":"WLC:Gen.41.56","status":"accepted","reason":"The same opening of stored food during famine is verified; the note does not claim Genesis has the Qur'an's post-famine pressing year.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.41.30","WLC:Gen.41.35","WLC:Gen.41.36","WLC:Gen.41.49","WLC:Gen.41.56"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-b228c4d095b2085b1c2b","ref":"WLC:Gen.50.20","status":"rejected","reason":"Joseph calls his brothers' evil an occasion for saving lives, but this later reconciliation does not describe grape pressing, medicine, or the stored-grain root.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.50.20"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-e67fefcc0c59431a92da","ref":"WLC:Isa.16.10","status":"rejected","reason":"A silenced winepress signals judgment on Moab, but the paragraph discusses a particular return of pressing after famine; Amos 9:13 gives the positive restoration scene.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.16.10"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-ce339e6e5c2627bf95a5","ref":"WLC:Isa.41.17","status":"accepted","reason":"The poor explicitly seek water and their tongues dry from thirst; the promise of an answer also makes relief part of the scene.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.41.17","WLC:Lam.4.4"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-ad3e0edeb2b420658dbf","ref":"WLC:Isa.41.17","status":"accepted","reason":"The second proposal's dried-tongue wording is exact in the opened Hebrew and represented with the Lamentations hunger scene.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.41.17","WLC:Lam.4.4"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-16418811203ef44ab67b","ref":"WLC:Isa.5.2","status":"rejected","reason":"The failed vineyard is an ethical motif, not a counter-narrative of the surah or Yusuf dream; the paired motif proposal is kept.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.5.2"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-1f470f6b7ba2df492490","ref":"WLC:Isa.5.2","status":"accepted","reason":"A winepress is prepared but good grapes fail, making the hoped-for good extract morally contingent; Isaiah 5:7 explains the fruit.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.5.2","WLC:Isa.5.7"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-fd7249d284e3a3dd1b3f","ref":"WLC:Isa.5.7","status":"accepted","reason":"The vineyard's failed fruit is interpreted as absent justice and righteousness; the annotation states this ethical interpretation.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.5.2","WLC:Isa.5.7"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-02c963e31d75102ed16e","ref":"WLC:Isa.58.10","status":"rejected","reason":"Giving one's resources to the hungry is costly generosity, but the verse uses no press, vessel, or extract and does not explain sabr.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.58.10"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-89f7104aeb27d566fb55","ref":"WLC:Isa.58.11","status":"rejected","reason":"A watered garden and unfailing spring portray restoration, but no squeezed source or dried tongue occurs in this verse; Isaiah 41:17 is more exact.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.58.11"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-18633cbd04d646241b95","ref":"WLC:Isa.63.3","status":"rejected","reason":"The wrath winepress does not retell a Qur'anic scene; also WLC 63:3 describes red spatter on garments rather than explicitly naming blood.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.63.3"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c2c25ec1531bed53f129","ref":"WLC:Isa.63.3","status":"rejected","reason":"This is a genuine wrath-press image, but Revelation 14:19-20 states the destructive blood-yield explicitly, while Isaiah 63:3 gives no separate feature for this short page.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.63.3"],"annotations":[]}
*** End Patch"));

