text(await tools.apply_patch("*** Begin Patch
*** Update File: enrichment/bible/work/s103/ehlikitap-images.sol.max.s103-20261009/sec3/verdicts.jsonl
@@
 {"connection_id":"BC-ed19bbb6df9a1ece151a","ref":"WLC:Zech.8.16","status":"rejected","reason":"Speaking truth to a neighbor is explicit, but the verse does not include patient endurance or reciprocal advice; 1 Thessalonians 5:14 joins communal correction and patience.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Zech.8.16"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.9.25","status":"accepted","reason":"The human who gains the whole world yet loses himself is a particularly close gain/loss question beside Qur'an 103:2.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.9.24","SBLGNT:Luke.9.25","SBLGNT:Phil.3.7","SBLGNT:Phil.3.8"],"annotations":["S103-INC-MTF-005"]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"SBLGNT:Phil.3.7","status":"accepted","reason":"Paul explicitly counts former gains as loss for Christ, a contrasting valuation stated in the selected gain/loss note.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.9.24","SBLGNT:Luke.9.25","SBLGNT:Phil.3.7","SBLGNT:Phil.3.8"],"annotations":["S103-INC-MTF-005"]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"SBLGNT:Phil.3.8","status":"accepted","reason":"The following verse repeats counting all as loss to gain Christ; it continues the annotation's deliberate reversal of profit and loss.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.9.24","SBLGNT:Luke.9.25","SBLGNT:Phil.3.7","SBLGNT:Phil.3.8"],"annotations":["S103-INC-MTF-005"]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"SBLGNT:Phil.3.9","status":"rejected","reason":"This context verse explains Paul's righteousness through faith but no longer uses the gain/loss terms selected from 3:7-8.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Phil.3.9"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"WLC:Esth.1.8","status":"rejected","reason":"The Hebrew אנס describes compulsion in drinking; despite sound correspondence to Arabic ء ن س, it is a semantic false friend to the page's word for human.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Esth.1.8"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"WLC:Dan.4.6","status":"rejected","reason":"Aramaic אנס means oppressive or difficult in the statement that no mystery troubles Daniel, not human or mortal.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.4.6"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"WLC:2Sam.22.12","status":"rejected","reason":"חשר denotes a mass of watery clouds; it is not חסר, the lack root, and does not explain the surah's human loss.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:2Sam.22.12"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"WLC:1Kgs.7.33","status":"rejected","reason":"חשר names wheel hubs, a gathering sense unrelated to lack, pressing, or medicinal storage.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:1Kgs.7.33"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"WLC:Gen.40.10","status":"accepted","reason":"The flowering vine and ripened grape clusters establish the actual dream context of the cupbearer's squeezing in 40:11.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.40.10","WLC:Gen.40.11","WLC:Gen.40.12","WLC:Gen.40.13"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-PRL-001"]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"WLC:Gen.40.12","status":"accepted","reason":"Joseph identifies the three branches as three days, required context for the restoration interpretation in 40:13.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.40.10","WLC:Gen.40.11","WLC:Gen.40.12","WLC:Gen.40.13"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-PRL-001"]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"WLC:Exod.15.24","status":"rejected","reason":"The people's question about what to drink supplies context to Marah but no distinct bitter-water or healing element beyond 15:23 and 15:25-26.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.15.24"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"WLC:Isa.5.4","status":"rejected","reason":"The vineyard owner repeats his disappointed grape expectation from 5:2; 5:7 supplies the distinct moral interpretation kept in the note.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.5.4"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"WLC:Joel.1.5","status":"rejected","reason":"The cut-off sweet wine is lamented during devastation, whereas Amos 9:13 gives pressing and returning abundance in one scene.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Joel.1.5"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"WLC:Joel.3.1","status":"accepted","reason":"God promises to pour his spirit upon all flesh; the selected note compares the same liquid-transfer form with the Qur'anic prayer for patience while distinguishing the gift.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Joel.3.1"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-006"]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"WLC:Joel.3.2","status":"rejected","reason":"The second verse repeats pouring spirit on servants and maidservants, adding recipients but no new liquid or verb distinction beyond 3:1.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Joel.3.2"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"WLC:Joel.4.18","status":"accepted","reason":"The mountains explicitly drip sweet wine in the restoration future, completing the abundance motif stated with Amos 9:13 and Joel 2:24.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Amos.9.13","WLC:Joel.2.24","WLC:Joel.4.18"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-005"]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"WLC:Hos.5.10","status":"rejected","reason":"Divine wrath poured like water is a punitive metaphor, not the saving patience poured on threatened believers; Joel 3:1 supplies the positive gift form.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Hos.5.10"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"WLC:Ezek.7.8","status":"rejected","reason":"God pours wrath on a condemned addressee, a distinct punitive action rather than an extract or a gift of endurance.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.7.8"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"SBLGNT:Acts.2.17","status":"rejected","reason":"Acts quotes Joel's spirit poured on all flesh; the original Hebrew scene is already kept, and Acts adds no patience-specific interpretation here.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.2.17"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"SBLGNT:Acts.2.18","status":"rejected","reason":"The continuation repeats the Joel spirit-outpouring for servants; no separate sabr or pressed-essence point is made.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.2.18"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.21.33","status":"rejected","reason":"The parable reuses Isaiah's vineyard with a dug winepress, but does not yet make the disappointed-grape and justice wordplay stated in Isaiah 5:2,7.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.21.33"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"SBLGNT:Rev.19.15","status":"rejected","reason":"The wrath winepress repeats Revelation 14:19-20 but adds no more explicit product than the blood named in 14:20.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Rev.19.15"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"CORPUSCORANICUM-INTERTEXT:103:2:178","status":"rejected","reason":"This editorial pointer identifies Luke 9's commercial loss image; the Greek Luke text was opened and kept directly, while its Syriac Peshitta cognate statement cannot be checked locally.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["CORPUSCORANICUM-INTERTEXT:103:2:178"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"CORPUSCORANICUM-INTERTEXT:103:2:179","status":"rejected","reason":"The editorial pointer to Philippians 3:7-8 led to the opened Greek verses; its Syriac wording is unavailable and its broad eschatological-damnation gloss overstates Paul's voluntary loss.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["CORPUSCORANICUM-INTERTEXT:103:2:179"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":null,"origin":"research","ref":"CORPUSCORANICUM:103:1-3:kommentar","status":"rejected","reason":"The modern note discusses late-afternoon oath and profit/loss, but contributes no primary biblical image beyond the separately opened Luke and Philippians passages.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["CORPUSCORANICUM:103:1-3:kommentar"],"annotations":[]}
*** End Patch"));

